outputs.L1:
A flexible pipe connector (200) for joining a pressurised mains pipe to a service pipe, the connector comprising:

- a corrugated longitudinal body portion (202) having a first end (208), a second end (210), and an internal bore extending therebetween, the longitudinal body portion further comprising a plurality of corrugations spaced along its length, the corrugations defining a maximum internal diameter of the bore at a corrugation peak (204) and a minimum internal diameter of the bore at a corrugation trough (206);
- a first spigot socket (212) at the first end of the longitudinal body portion; and
- a second spigot socket (214) at the second end of the longitudinal body portion;

wherein, for a defined volumetric flow rate, the head loss of a fluid flowing through the body portion is no greater than the head loss that would occur if the fluid were flowing through a smooth-bore pipe of the same length and having the same internal diameter as the first and second spigot sockets; and

wherein the minimum internal diameter of the corrugated internal bore is greater than the maximum internal diameter of the first and second spigots.

counterfactuals.output:
**1. A flexible pipe connector for fluidly coupling a pressurised mains pipe to a service pipe, the connector comprising:**  

a flexible tubular body extending along a longitudinal axis between a mains end and a service end, the tubular body including a corrugated load-bearing wall configured to permit bending of the connector while withstanding an internal mains pressure;  

a fluid-impermeable inner liner supported within the corrugated load-bearing wall and defining a substantially smooth, continuous flow bore between the mains end and the service end, the inner liner being configured to flex with the tubular body without forming corrugations projecting into the flow bore;  

a mains coupling at the mains end configured to form a pressure-tight connection with the pressurised mains pipe; and  

a service coupling at the service end configured to form a pressure-tight connection with the service pipe,  

wherein transitions between the mains coupling, the substantially smooth flow bore, and the service coupling are substantially free of flow-obstructing discontinuities, and wherein the flow bore has a hydraulic diameter and surface profile selected such that, at a specified fluid flow rate, the connector produces a head loss no greater than that produced by an equivalent smooth-bore pipe having the same effective flow length and hydraulic diameter.

Flexible pipe connector **200** is described as having:

- an elongated, flexible **body portion 202** formed with successive corrugations;
- each corrugation having an outward **peak 204** and an intervening **trough 206**, allowing the body to bend, extend, or compress without collapsing;
- opposite terminal **ends 208 and 210**; and
- respective **spigot sockets 212 and 214** at those ends for receiving and sealing around pipe spigots, thereby connecting two pipe sections.

Hydraulically, the connector is claimed to maintain an open, substantially full-bore flow passage while flexed. The corrugated geometry resists kinking or flattening, and the sockets provide sealed transitions to the adjoining pipes. Consequently, the connector is said to preserve useful flow capacity with comparatively low obstruction, turbulence, and pressure/head loss, while accommodating misalignment or movement between connected pipes.
